  <Fragment slot="content">
    <div class="rcc-beam-steel-content prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
      <h2>RCC Beam Steel Calculation Formulas & Detailing Rules (IS 456 & IS 13920)</h2>
      <p>
        Reinforced Cement Concrete (RCC) beams are primary horizontal structural members designed to withstand bending moments (sagging at mid-span, hogging at supports) and vertical shear forces (maximum near columns/supports). Accurate determination of the <strong>Bar Bending Schedule (BBS)</strong> and <strong>concrete takeoff</strong> prevents site shortages, eliminates rebar cutting scrap, and ensures strict conformity with building codes.
      </p>

      <h3>1. Standard Steel Unit Weight Formula (D² / 162)</h3>
      <p>
        The nominal weight per running meter of any cylindrical steel rebar is derived from the density of mild/TMT steel (<code>ρ = 7850 kg/m³</code>):
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-100 p-3 rounded-xl font-mono text-sm dark:bg-slate-800">
        Weight (kg/m) = (Diameter in mm)² ÷ 162.28 ≈ D² ÷ 162
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>8 mm:</strong> 0.395 kg/m</li>
        <li><strong>10 mm:</strong> 0.617 kg/m</li>
        <li><strong>12 mm:</strong> 0.889 kg/m</li>
        <li><strong>16 mm:</strong> 1.580 kg/m</li>
        <li><strong>20 mm:</strong> 2.469 kg/m</li>
        <li><strong>25 mm:</strong> 3.858 kg/m</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2. Main Reinforcement Cutting Length Formula</h3>
      <p>
        The cutting length of straight continuous through bars anchored into column or beam supports:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-100 p-3 rounded-xl font-mono text-sm dark:bg-slate-800">
        Cutting Length = Clear Span + 2 × (Support Bearing Width - End Cover) + 2 × (Hook Length) - Bend Deductions
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Standard 90° Hook:</strong> Typically <code>12d</code> to <code>16d</code> (minimum 150 mm).</li>
        <li><strong>Development Length (Ld):</strong> For full tension anchorage as per IS 456 Cl. 26.2.1, <code>Ld ≈ 48d</code> for Fe500 in M20 concrete.</li>
        <li><strong>Bend Deductions (IS 2502):</strong> Deduct <code>2d</code> for each 90° bend to account for elongation of steel rebar during cold bending.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>3. Stirrups (Shear Links) Cutting Length & Hooks (IS 2502 & IS 13920)</h3>
      <p>
        A standard rectangular beam ring enclosing the top and bottom main rebar cage:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-100 p-3 rounded-xl font-mono text-sm dark:bg-slate-800">
        Core Width (a) = Beam Width - 2 × Clear Cover<br/>
        Core Depth (b) = Beam Depth - 2 × Clear Cover<br/>
        Cutting Length = 2 × (a + b) + 2 × (Hook Length) - Bend Deductions
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>135° Seismic Hook Length:</strong> Must be <code>10 × bar diameter</code> (minimum 75 mm) as mandated by IS 13920:2016 for earthquake-resistant ductile detailing.</li>
        <li><strong>Bend Deductions:</strong> 3 bends of 90° (<code>3 × 2d = 6d</code>) + 2 bends of 135° (<code>2 × 3d = 6d</code>) = <code>12d</code> total deduction.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>4. Two-Zone Shear Detailing (IS 13920 Ductile Detailing)</h3>
      <p>
        Shear force is highest near support faces and diminishes towards mid-span. Ductile earthquake detailing (IS 13920) divides the beam into:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Support Zones (L/4 or 2D at each end):</strong> Closely spaced stirrups (e.g. 100 mm c/c) to arrest diagonal tension cracks and provide core concrete confinement.</li>
        <li><strong>Mid-Span Zone (remaining L/2):</strong> Wider spacing (e.g. 150 mm or 200 mm c/c) where bending moment dominates and shear stress is minimal.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>5. Side Face Reinforcement (IS 456 Clause 26.5.1.6)</h3>
      <p>
        When the overall depth of a beam exceeds <strong>750 mm</strong>, side face (skin) reinforcement of not less than <strong>0.1% of the web area</strong> must be distributed equally on both vertical faces with spacing not exceeding 300 mm or web width. This prevents lateral web buckling and controls shrinkage/thermal cracking.
      </p>

      <h3>6. Site Thumb-Rule Rates (CPWD Preliminary Estimation)</h3>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800">
            <tr>
              <th class="p-2 border">Beam Classification</th>
              <th class="p-2 border">Steel Ratio (% Concrete Vol)</th>
              <th class="p-2 border">Steel Consumption Rate</th>
              <th class="p-2 border">Typical Applications</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-2 border font-bold">Light / Tie Beam</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">1.0%</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">80 kg / m³</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">Plinth beams, lintel tie beams, roof parapet</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-2 border font-bold">Standard Floor Beam</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">1.5%</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">120 kg / m³</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">Residential G+1, G+2 floor and roof beams</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-2 border font-bold">Heavy Commercial Beam</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">2.0%</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">160 kg / m³</td>
              <td class="p-2 border">Commercial complexes, basement transfer girders</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>7. Shuttering / Formwork Calculation Formula</h3>
      <p>
        The shuttering contact area for a cast-in-place beam supported by columns comprises the bottom soffit plus both exposed vertical sides:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-100 p-3 rounded-xl font-mono text-sm dark:bg-slate-800">
        Shuttering Area (m²) = Total Length × (Beam Width + 2 × Beam Depth)
      </div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- FAQS WITH SCHEMA JSON-LD (SLOT FAQ) -->
  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open>
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          What is the thumb rule for steel in an RCC beam?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          The standard structural engineering thumb rule (CPWD / IS 456) for RCC beams is <strong>1.0% to 2.0% steel reinforcement by volume of concrete</strong>. This translates to roughly <strong>80 kg to 160 kg of steel per cubic meter (m³) of concrete</strong>. For standard residential floor beams (230×450 mm), 120 kg/m³ is the most common preliminary estimation benchmark.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          How do you calculate the cutting length of a rectangular stirrup ring?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          <code>Cutting Length = 2 × (Core Width + Core Depth) + 2 × (Hook Length) - Bend Deductions</code>. Core width is <code>Width - 2 × Clear Cover</code>, and core depth is <code>Depth - 2 × Clear Cover</code>. For 135° seismic hooks, each hook is <code>10d</code> (min 75mm), and total bend deduction for three 90° bends plus two 135° bends is <code>12d</code> (IS 2502).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          Why are stirrups placed closer together near the column supports?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          According to IS 13920:2016 ductile detailing provisions, shear force in a loaded beam is maximum at the face of supports and near zero at mid-span. Placing stirrups closer (e.g. 100 mm c/c) over a distance of <code>L/4</code> or <code>2D</code> from the support face prevents catastrophic diagonal shear tension failure and confines the concrete during earthquakes.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          When is side face reinforcement required in an RCC beam?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Under IS 456:2000 Clause 26.5.1.6, side face reinforcement is mandatory when the total depth of the beam exceeds <strong>750 mm</strong> (or 450 mm when the beam is subjected to significant torsion). The minimum area required is 0.1% of the web area, divided equally on both faces at a spacing not exceeding 300 mm.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          How many commercial 12-meter rebar rods are in a bundle?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Standard factory mill packaging for commercial 12-meter (40-foot) rebars: <strong>8 mm = 10 rods/bundle</strong>, <strong>10 mm = 7 rods/bundle</strong>, <strong>12 mm = 5 rods/bundle</strong>, <strong>16 mm = 3 rods/bundle</strong>, <strong>20 mm = 2 rods/bundle</strong>, and <strong>25 mm = 1 rod</strong>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
          How is binding wire quantity calculated for RCC beams?
          <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Civil engineering site practice requires approximately <strong>10 kg to 12 kg of 18-gauge annealed GI binding wire per metric tonne of rebar</strong> (roughly 1% of total steel weight) to tie main reinforcement and stirrups securely.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
